Ancient Greek I: A 21st Century Approach (@greek-source-ancient-greek: 0, is #book, #part-of @greek-language-sources) { r_1D87YRHMAM7FHZH3VCDY9JTHGX
Philip S. Peek. Ancient Greek I: A 21st Century Approach. Cambridge: Open Book Publishers, 2021. English instruction with Ancient Greek reading. EPUB ISBN 9781800642577. DOI 10.11647/OBP.0264.

[Open textbook, reading formats, and licence](https://www.openbookpublishers.com/books/10.11647/obp.0264). Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International.

The thirty-module textbook introduces the alphabet, sounds, accents, endings, vocabulary, and the grammatical analysis used to read ancient sentences. Begin with modules 1–3, then work through the sequence. Module 6 introduces how endings create meaning; the later modules develop the functions needed for sustained reading.

**Use with:** [Reading a short philosophical passage in Greek](../languages/Read-original-texts.lingua#r_1Q07KPDNG2JTY7HRWQY5H6T5MJ). A first comparison may use one clause alongside a translation. Independent reading of whole dialogues needs the subsequent grammar and vocabulary.

**Return:** [Ancient Greek](../languages/Ancient-Greek.lingua#r_1XJQDKNZ29XC8S5CD8GS4YK3EH) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).

**Further language work:** [Smyth's substantial grammar](Greek-Grammar-Smyth.lingua#r_1HWQDW2XAJNQAXTWS9P0QT57DG) supports the constructions required by the research passage. The [textual dossier](../research/Philological-study.lingua#r_353E4E4WH3BWFHHJNZMPKBPEXF) defines the ability to demonstrate.
} r_1D87YRHMAM7FHZH3VCDY9JTHGX
